From: GitHub Copilot <copilot@github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: GTK Wayland window visibility - send frame callbacks on mapped views

This patch fixes an issue where GTK applications (and other Wayland clients)
would launch but fail to display their windows when using pure Wayland with
Hikari compositor.

The root cause: The renderer's damage frame handler was returning early without
rendering or committing output state when there was no visual damage. This meant
that GTK applications, which wait for frame callbacks before rendering, would
never receive those callbacks and thus never display their windows.

The fix ensures that even when there's no damage, if there are mapped views on
the output, we still commit the output state and send frame callbacks to all
clients. This allows Wayland applications to properly synchronize their
rendering with the compositor.

Fixes: https://codeberg.org/thomasadam/hikari/issues/10

---
 src/renderer.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/renderer.c b/src/renderer.c
index 71a2793..xxxx 100644
--- a/src/renderer.c
+++ b/src/renderer.c
@@ -673,10 +673,17 @@ hikari_renderer_damage_frame_handler(
 
   struct wlr_output *wlr_output = output->wlr_output;
   bool has_damage = pixman_region32_not_empty(&output->damage.current);
+  
+  /* Check if there are any views on this output that need frame callbacks.
+   * Even without visual damage, we must commit frames for mapped views to
+   * ensure they receive the frame callbacks they're waiting for. This is
+   * critical for Wayland clients like GTK that synchronize rendering with
+   * the compositor's frame cycle. */
+  bool has_views = !wl_list_empty(&output->views);
 
-  if (!wlr_output->needs_frame && !has_damage) {
+  if (!wlr_output->needs_frame && !has_damage && !has_views) {
     frame_done(output);
     return;
   }
 
   pixman_region32_t buffer_damage;
